{{also|Spritz}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/spɹɪts/|/ʃpɹɪts/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪts|s=1}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-spritz.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{bor|en|yi|שפּריץ|pos=noun}} and {{m|yi|שפּריצן|pos=verb}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A [[sprinkling]] or [[spray]] of [[liquid]]; a small amount of liquid.
#: {{ux|en|Fish is good with a '''spritz''' of lemon juice.}}

=====Translations=====
{{trans-top|sprinkling or spray of liquid}}
* German: {{t+|de|Spritzer|m}}, {{t+|de|Schuss|m}}
{{trans-bottom}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# To [[spray]], [[sprinkle]], or [[squirt]] lightly.
#: {{ux|en|It's hard to keep hair in a specific hairdo when you just '''spritz''' only a little bit of hair spray.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Benjamin Nugent|title=Good Kids|year=2013|publisher=Simon and Schuster|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=qcBadP2X4dIC|isbn=978-1-4391-5433-5|passage=“Sometimes,” he hastened to explain, looking at Khadijah and me, stroking his beard, “the fruit at Gaia is slightly rotten. That's the dark side of organic. Everything isn't '''spritzed''' with poisons to make it look neat.”}}
# {{lb|en|impersonal|weather}} To [[drizzle]], to [[rain]] lightly.

=====Translations=====
{{trans-top|to spray, sprinkle, or squirt lightly}}
* German: {{t+|de|sprühen}}, {{t+|de|spritzen}}
{{trans-bottom}}

